From: Veron <veron@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] tccpp: decimal float literals must land in a local first

WHAT BREAKS. A tcc produced by this bootstrap chain parses decimal floating
literals into the wrong value. Measured with the compiler the hermetic amd64
ladder actually built (work/ref-tcc, from the compiler-under-test artifact),
compiling `double x = 0.5;`:

    literal 0.5    ->  0x1.9796cp-35     wrong
    literal 1.5    ->  0x1.40d178p-33    wrong
    literal 0.1    ->  0x1.39ad08p-34    wrong
    literal 123456789.0 -> 0x1.4af7p-10  wrong

while 0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, 10.0 and 1024.0 come out right, and so does the
HEX spelling of the same numbers: 0x1p-1 gives exactly 0.5.

WHY IT MATTERS. gcc's real.c has

    #define M_LOG10_2 0.30102999566398119521

and a gcc built by such a tcc carries 1.266209359584991e-12 in its .rodata
instead. Every decimal exponent it estimates is then nonsense, and cc1 dies at
real.c:1724 on the first float constant it is asked to print -- which is what
`cc1 -E -dM /dev/null` does, and therefore what configure does, and therefore
where rung 6 has been failing.

WHAT IS NOT AT FAULT, each ruled out by test rather than by reading:

  * musl's strtod. floatscan.o was lifted out of the ladder's own libc.a and
    linked into a gcc-built musl in its place; every value came back correct,
    including 0.5, 0.1 and 123456789.0.
  * x87 long double arithmetic. divide, multiply, an accumulating loop and
    (long double)unsigned all produce bit-identical results under ref-tcc and
    under gcc.
  * the target model. sizeof(long double) is 16, and musl's own configure
    agrees the compiler's definition matches float.h.
  * the tcc source. A tcc built from this exact patched tree by a host gcc
    emits 0.30102999566398119521 correctly, and so does a tcc built by THAT
    tcc. x86_64 self-hosting is clean.

WHAT IS AT FAULT. The bug is in the ref-tcc BINARY, put there by the compiler
that built it -- x86_64-tcc, which runs on aarch64 and is itself built by
mc-tcc. The two literal paths differ in exactly one way:

  hex      d = strtod(hbuf, 0);  ...  tokc.d = (double)d;   via a local, works
  decimal  tokc.d = strtod(token_buf, NULL);                direct, broken

Both call the same working strtod. Only the direct assignment of the returned
double into the CValue union is mishandled.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It routes every decimal literal
through a local of the right type before the union assignment, which is what
the hex path already does and what already works. It is a WORKAROUND: the
defect is in x86_64-tcc's code generation and is still there, waiting for
anything else that assigns a floating return value straight into a struct or
union member. Finding that is tracked separately; this exists so the ladder can
get past rung 6 while it is found.

--- a/tccpp.c
+++ b/tccpp.c
@@ -2514,18 +2514,22 @@
             if (t == 'F') {
                 ch = *p++;
                 tok = TOK_CFLOAT;
-                tokc.f = strtof(token_buf, NULL);
+                /* THROUGH A LOCAL. Assigning a floating return value straight
+                   into the CValue union is miscompiled by the compiler that
+                   builds this one; the hex path above already stores into a
+                   local first and is correct. See the patch header. */
+                { float fv = strtof(token_buf, NULL); tokc.f = fv; }
             } else if (t == 'L') {
                 ch = *p++;
                 tok = TOK_CLDOUBLE;
 #ifdef TCC_USING_DOUBLE_FOR_LDOUBLE
-                tokc.d = strtod(token_buf, NULL);
+                { double dv = strtod(token_buf, NULL); tokc.d = dv; }
 #else
-                tokc.ld = strtold(token_buf, NULL);
+                { long double lv = strtold(token_buf, NULL); tokc.ld = lv; }
 #endif
             } else {
                 tok = TOK_CDOUBLE;
-                tokc.d = strtod(token_buf, NULL);
+                { double dv = strtod(token_buf, NULL); tokc.d = dv; }
             }
         }
     } else {
